"L1": {
"code": "L1",
"title": "LEDs stay dark",
"family": "LED ring",
"kind": "Studio-guided",
"detect": "C0 lights the ring white at low brightness for 2 s and asks what you see",
"body": "**Causes, most likely first:**\n1. Wired to the strip's **far** end. Data only goes in at the input end (the arrows point away from it).\n2. No common ground between the LED supply and the ESP32 (build guide Step 39.3).\n3. Level shifter pin 1 (1OE) not tied to GND, or pin 14 (VCC) not on 5V.\n4. No 5 V reaching the strip: blown fuse, or a switched-off supply.\n5. Wrong strip type: 12 V, or a plain RGB strip with R, G, B pads (see doc 03).\n6. D1 not on GPIO4 (left pin 4), or D2 not on chip pin 3.\n\n**Fix:**\n1. Check the arrows: your green data wire must be on the end they point **away** from.\n2. Check the ground wire from ESP32 right-header pin 21 to the GND rail.\n3. Check chip pins 1 and 14 (build guide Step 37).\n4. Check the fuse, and that the supply is on (most have a small light).\n5. Check the strip's pad labels say 5V, DIN and GND.\n6. **Test again.**"
},
"L2": {
"code": "L2",
"title": "Random colours or flicker",
"family": "LED ring",
"kind": "Symptom",
"detect": "",
"body": "**Causes:**\n1. No level shifter (the direct-wire stopgap).\n2. A long data wire (over 15 cm from the chip to the strip).\n3. The 330 Ω resistor missing.\n4. A weak ground.\n5. The capacitor missing.\n\n**Fix:** fit the 74AHCT125 and the resistor, keep the data wire short, check the ground, and fit the capacitor. **Test again.**"
},
"L3": {
"code": "L3",
"title": "Wrong colours (red shows as green)",
"family": "LED ring",
"kind": "Studio-guided",
"detect": "C0 shows red, green and blue in turn, and asks what you see",
"body": "**Fix:** answer the colour test, and the studio sets the strip's colour order automatically."
},
